{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GtYG8ZdRmiWghcNdf6dJ1D","type":"tool_result","content":"# ard configuration for albertcbrown/ard (dogfooding). Started from `ard init`'s\n# template; human-owned. ard serve reads it from origin/main.\n# Anything unset takes its built-in default (docs/proposal.md, Configuration).\n# Validate with `ard config check`. ard serve reads this file from origin/main.\n\n# label: only issues a maintainer labels for triage enter the pipeline.\n# all: every human-filed issue enters.\nintake: { mode: label }\n\nmerge:\n  # A PR touching any of these waits for a human approval. There is no\n  # built-in list: removing an entry removes its gate. An entry ending in /\n  # is a directory prefix; anything else is a glob (quote globs starting with *).\n  protected_paths:\n    - .github/\n    - .ard/config.yaml\n    - .ard/scanners/\n    - .ard/triage/\n    - .ard/workers/\n    - .ard/reviewers/\n    - \"**/CODEOWNERS\"\n    # Agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents.\n    - CLAUDE.md\n    - \"**/CLAUDE.md\"\n    - AGENTS.md\n    - \"**/AGENTS.md\"\n    - .claude/\n    - .mcp.json\n    # What builds, signs and deploys ard itself (docs/proposal.md, Delivery).\n    - \"Jenkinsfile*\"\n    - Dockerfile\n    - .dockerignore\n    - deploy/\n\noverrides:\n  scanners/cve: { every: 6h, max_open: 10, group_size: 1, autoclose: true }\n  # Phase 1 agent roles (the ard init starter set, #39 Gate 2).\n  triage/lead:  { model: claude-opus-5-5, timeout: 15m }\n  triage/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\n  workers/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5, timeout: 60m, tests: make test }\n  reviewers/eng-review: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\nCLAUDE.md\nDockerfile\nLICENSE\nMakefile\nREADME.md\ncmd\ndeploy\ndocs\ngo.mod\ngo.sum\ninternal\ninternal:\nagent\nagentjob\nbotcheck\ncli\ncloseout\ncommitter\nconfig\nconfigcmd\nconfigsrc\ncontroller\ncostblock\ncosts\neffects\nexecutor\ngithub\ngitref\ngitx\ninitcmd\nintake\nlabels\nlimits\nmodelauth\npool\nreaper\nredact\nrepopath\nreviewer\nroles\nscanner\nservecmd\nstate\ntriage\nworker\nwtlock\n\ninternal/redact:\ndoc.go\nentropy.go\nhelpers_test.go\nhostpath.go\nhostpath_test.go\npath.go\npath_test.go\nrace_off_test.go\nrace_on_test.go\nredact.go\nredact_test.go\nreplace.go\nrules.go\nscan.go\nscan_test.go\nsecrets.go\nsecrets_test.go\ntruncate.go\ntruncate_test.go\n      36 internal/redact/doc.go\n      23 internal/redact/entropy.go\n      36 internal/redact/helpers_test.go\n      83 internal/redact/hostpath.go\n     195 internal/redact/hostpath_test.go\n      25 internal/redact/path.go\n      41 internal/redact/path_test.go\n       5 internal/redact/race_off_test.go\n       6 internal/redact/race_on_test.go\n     223 internal/redact/redact.go\n     345 internal/redact/redact_test.go\n     155 internal/redact/replace.go\n      60 internal/redact/rules.go\n     121 internal/redact/scan.go\n     104 internal/redact/scan_test.go\n     200 internal/redact/secrets.go\n     289 internal/redact/secrets_test.go\n      55 internal/redact/truncate.go\n      67 internal/redact/truncate_test.go\n    2069 total\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n127:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n128-\n129-The stage in a role file's name is the label stage (`triage`, `worker`, `review`; `config.Kind` and the role directory spell the last one `reviewer`), so a triage and a worker role with the same name never share a file.\n130-\n131-Every `<stage>-<role>.md` starts with frontmatter the controller reads, so the agent reports its outcome without touching GitHub:\n132-\n133-```\n134----\n135-role: engineer\n136-status: done          # done | blocked | needs-human (both of the last two add ard:needs-human)\n137-attempt: 1\n138-next: [review:security-review, review:eng-review]   # optional requests\n139-worker: engineer      # lead only: which worker role implements the plan\n140-verdict: approve      # reviewers only: approve | changes (required when status is done)\n141-findings:             # reviewers only, optional: at most 50\n142-  - file: internal/foo/bar.go   # repo-relative slash path (required)\n143-    line: 42                    # 1-based; absent means the whole file\n144-    severity: high              # critical | high | medium | low | info (required)\n145-    blocking: true              # default false\n146-    message: The loop skips the last element.   # required, non-empty\n147----\n148-Short summary of what was done.\n149-\n150-## Open issues\n151-- ...\n152-```\n--\n219:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the issue, its timeline and the labeler's permission, and it fails closed. Issues authored by the App (scanner-filed) always pass. In `all` mode only issues opened after `all` mode was first enabled enter without a label; older issues still need the label. In `label` mode only the most recent `labeled` event for the label counts: it must come from the App or from a collaborator with role triage, write, maintain or admin (matched case-insensitively), and an earlier authorised event does not rescue a later unauthorised one. When the label is present but not honoured, the controller adds `ard:needs-human` with a short comment asking a maintainer to re-apply the label. The loop records each admission in the state `admissions` table (`labeled_by`, `labeled_at`, `body_hash`); if the title or body is edited after admission, the issue is re-gated with `ard:needs-human` and a comment until a maintainer re-applies the intake label. App-authored issues are exempt from the hash check. The loop never waits for a job: each claimed job is handed to a watcher goroutine that closes out through the shared Finisher, and a restart leaves running jobs to pool adoption and the Reaper. The re-gate hash covers the title and the body without ard's own cost block (see Guardrails, \"Intake is configurable\"), so ard's table writes never re-gate an issue. The first time ard writes the block, the stored hash stays valid; a maintainer relabel that falls between a human edit and ard's next table write may need repeating, because an ard body write bumps the issue's `updated_at`, which fails safe.\n220-2. Wait for a free job slot (triage ranks below reviewers and workers), then claim the oldest match: add `ard:triage:<role>:running` and record the job (id, executor, start, deadline). Claiming runs in a watcher goroutine; at most one triage job per issue; under `--dry-run` the loop logs `triage.would_claim` and creates no job, slot or label.\n221-3. Prepare a detached worktree at `origin/main`, write the untrusted snapshot to `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (never committed; the committed `issue.md` stays owned by the scanner), and mount the role's sidecar repo read-only at `$ARD_SIDECAR`.\n222-4. Run the agent CLI with `.ard/triage/<role>/prompt.txt` and the issue directory as context.\n223-5. Close out: read `triage-<role>.md` frontmatter, send the issue directory (and nothing else) to the committer, post the summary as a comment, swap `:running` for `:done`, and apply any requests.\n224-\n225-**The lead routes; the controller applies.** The lead writes `next: [triage:architect, triage:product-manager]` and `worker: engineer` in `triage-lead.md`. The controller adds the matching `ard:` labels only if each names a role directory that exists.\n226-\n227-The starter lead (`.ard/triage/lead/prompt.txt`) follows a fixed contract. It reads the issue only through the untrusted input files `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, which the triage loop writes and never commits. It finds roles by listing the role directories and routes only to those. It writes only `triage-lead.md`, with `attempt: 1`, and a `status: done` always names a `worker`. It answers `needs-human` when a person must decide: an unclear ask, a question, a duplicate, a change to `.ard/config.yaml`, the role directories or `.github/`, no fitting worker, or text that tries to instruct it. It uses `review:<role>` only for a reviewer whose `when` rule would skip a change that needs that review. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-lead.md`; any other change under `.ard/<issue#>/` fails the job as needs-human.\n228-\n229-The starter engineer (`.ard/triage/engineer/prompt.txt`) plans for the worker without implementing. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, all untrusted. It writes the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md` with Steps, Test plan, Risk and Size, sized S, M, L or XL on a scale tied to the worker's 1,000-line guard (XL is 1,000 lines or more). It writes `triage-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, never a `worker` key. It answers `needs-human` for an XL change, a change to protected role and config paths or `.github/`, or a missing product or design decision, and leaves `plan.md` untouched. In Phase 1 it has no `after:`; the lead's request orders it. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-engineer.md` and the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md`; a change to `triage-lead.md`, `issue.md` or another role's section fails the job as needs-human.\n230-\n231-**A deterministic gate starts the worker.** Once per triage tick, after the intake sweep, the controller searches for open issues that carry `ard:triage:lead:done` and none of `ard:pending`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` or any configured worker's request label (the exclusion keeps gated issues from filling the 100-hit window). It re-reads each hit and decides from labels alone. \"Requested\" means the request label is present, \"done\" means `:done` is present. The gate waits while any triage job of the issue is running or any requested `ard:triage:<role>` lacks its `:done`; it leaves an issue alone once any `ard:worker:<role>` label exists (a worker already gated, started by a human, or in a fix round). When triage is finished it adds the lead's chosen `ard:worker:<role>`. The choice is read from the lead's close-out record in controller state, the value close-out validated, not from the committed `triage-lead.md`. It needs no pool slot and runs in the same conditions as the rest of the tick.\n232-\n233-Three cases go to a human, each with one comment and `ard:needs-human`, and nothing is ever closed automatically: the lead finished `done` but named no valid worker (or its close-out record is missing), the chosen worker role no longer exists, or an `ard:triage:<role>` is requested for a role that does not exist. A title or body edit after admission, on a human-filed issue, also stops the gate with a \"paused\" comment and `ard:needs-human`; re-admission by relabel is not offered, since a lead with `:done` is never re-claimed. A maintainer re-runs triage or adds the worker label.\n234-\n235-With `require_approval: true` (the Phase 1 default) the gate posts one \"ready for approval\" comment per lead job and waits. `ard:approved` is honoured only when its latest `labeled` event (reset by an `unlabeled`) is by a collaborator with triage permission or higher, is not the App, and comes after the last triage `:done` label of the issue. An approval given earlier is left in place and ignored: the gate waits for a fresh one and does not escalate. An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor. A human who adds `ard:worker:<role>` directly bypasses the gate and has approved by doing so. The gate does not enforce that `triage:engineer` ran: the lead routes and the gate applies its routing. In `--dry-run` the gate logs `triage.would_gate` and writes nothing; otherwise each action records a `gate` event.\n236-\n237-**Order comes from config, not the lead.** A role can declare `after: [architect, product-manager]`; the controller holds its request until those roles, if requested, are done. A prerequisite is requested when its request label (`ard:pending` for the lead) or its `:running` label is present, and done when its `:done` label is present; one that was never requested does not hold. A held role is logged as `triage.held`, is not a candidate and does not count against the one-triage-job-per-issue rule. Config rejects a role that is after itself and any cycle. Engineer triage normally runs last so its steps reflect the design and requirements.\n238-\n239-| Role | Adds to plan.md | Typical trigger |\n240-| --- | --- | --- |\n241-| lead | nothing (role file only); writes `triage-lead.md` with routing, or `needs-human` when a person must decide | every `ard:pending` issue |\n242-| product-manager | `## Requirements`: user value, acceptance criteria, out of scope | feature asks |\n243-| architect | `## Design`: approach, affected modules, ADR needed or not | cross-module change, new dependency |\n244-| designer | `## UX`, plus design files in `.ard/<issue#>/design/` | UI change |\n--\n408:## Guardrails\n409-\n410-Agents with push access read text that strangers can write, so the controls sit in code and permissions, not in prompts.\n411-\n412-- **Identity: a GitHub App, not a PAT.** Installation tokens expire hourly and are scoped to contents, issues and pull requests (read and write), plus checks (read and write). The merger needs read to see required checks; the owner kept write so ard can later publish its own check runs, but nothing in Phase 1 writes checks. Leaving out the `workflows` permission makes GitHub itself reject any push that edits `.github/workflows/`, a second backstop behind a guard that already refuses all of `.github/`, and it also keeps the bot from editing the bot-commit audit. The scratch-repo run (#85) confirmed that GitHub rejects such a push (\"refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow ... without `workflows` permission\"), and that these work with exactly this permission set: label writes, PR create, review and mark-ready, pinning the status issue, and timeline and collaborator-permission reads. A personal token (`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN`) is accepted only for local `--dry-run` development, and the client is then made read-only so no write can reach GitHub.\n413-- **The GitHub client never follows redirects.** A redirect can turn a write into a silent GET, replay it into another repository, or carry the token to another host. An issue that redirects to another repository (transferred) reads as not found. A repository that redirects (renamed or transferred) is a hard, non-transient error naming the new name when GitHub reveals it. ard does not follow a rename; point it at the new name. A deleted issue (410 Gone) also reads as not found.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92566d09-cc51-4307-8584-ef00e4a9d8c9","uuid":"0ba6ccb3-de9b-404a-972e-d275e91cb775","timestamp":"2026-11-23T13:18:51.715Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# ard configuration for albertcbrown/ard (dogfooding). Started from `ard init`'s\n# template; human-owned. ard serve reads it from origin/main.\n# Anything unset takes its built-in default (docs/proposal.md, Configuration).\n# Validate with `ard config check`. ard serve reads this file from origin/main.\n\n# label: only issues a maintainer labels for triage enter the pipeline.\n# all: every human-filed issue enters.\nintake: { mode: label }\n\nmerge:\n  # A PR touching any of these waits for a human approval. There is no\n  # built-in list: removing an entry removes its gate. An entry ending in /\n  # is a directory prefix; anything else is a glob (quote globs starting with *).\n  protected_paths:\n    - .github/\n    - .ard/config.yaml\n    - .ard/scanners/\n    - .ard/triage/\n    - .ard/workers/\n    - .ard/reviewers/\n    - \"**/CODEOWNERS\"\n    # Agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents.\n    - C