{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HhfewuBws3QkfYdtzun66G","type":"tool_result","content":"package redact\n\nimport (\n\t\"bufio\"\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n)\n\n// Finding is one scanner hit. It never carries the matched text.\ntype Finding struct {\n\tRule string // rule ID, e.g. \"github-token\"\n\tLine int    // 1-based line in the scanned stream\n\tCol  int    // 1-based byte column\n}\n\n// Scanner looks for secrets in a stream. Implementations must fail closed:\n// return an error rather than a partial verdict.\ntype Scanner interface {\n\tScan(ctx context.Context, r io.Reader) ([]Finding, error)\n}\n\nconst (\n\tscanWindow     = 1 << 20\n\tscanOverlap    = 4 << 10\n\tmaxScanReports = 10000\n)\n\ntype builtin struct{}\n\n// Builtin returns the in-repo pattern scanner. It stores at most 10000\n// findings per scan.\nfunc Builtin() Scanner { return builtin{} }\n\nfunc (builtin) Scan(ctx context.Context, r io.Reader) ([]Finding, error) {\n\tvar out []Finding\n\ttype key struct {\n\t\trule      string\n\t\tline, col int\n\t}\n\tseen := map[key]bool{}\n\temit := func(f Finding) {\n\t\tk := key{f.Rule, f.Line, f.Col}\n\t\tif !seen[k] && len(out) < maxScanReports {\n\t\t\tseen[k] = true\n\t\t\tout = append(out, f)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tbr := bufio.NewReaderSize(r, scanWindow)\n\tline, consumed, iter := 1, 0, 0\n\tvar carry []byte\n\tfor {\n\t\titer++\n\t\tif iter%1000 == 0 || carry != nil {\n\t\t\tif err := ctx.Err(); err != nil {\n\t\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"scan: %w\", err)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tfrag, err := br.ReadSlice('\\n')\n\t\tif err != nil && err != bufio.ErrBufferFull && err != io.EOF {\n\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"scan: read: %w\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tlong := err == bufio.ErrBufferFull\n\t\tdata, base := frag, consumed\n\t\tif carry != nil {\n\t\t\tdata = append(append([]byte(nil), carry...), frag...)\n\t\t\tbase = consumed - len(carry)\n\t\t}\n\t\tif len(data) > 0 {\n\t\t\tscanLine(bytes.TrimRight(data, \"\\r\\n\"), line, base, emit)\n\t\t}\n\t\tconsumed += len(frag)\n\t\tif long {\n\t\t\tfrom := len(data) - scanOverlap\n\t\t\tif from < 0 {\n\t\t\t\tfrom = 0\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tcarry = append([]byte(nil), data[from:]...)\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tcarry, consumed = nil, 0\n\t\tif err == io.EOF {\n\t\t\treturn out, nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tline++\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc scanLine(data []byte, line, base int, emit func(Finding)) {\n\tfor i := range rules {\n\t\tru := &rules[i]\n\t\tif ru.generic {\n\t\t\tif bytes.IndexByte(data, ':') < 0 && bytes.IndexByte(data, '=') < 0 {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tfor _, m := range ru.re.FindAllSubmatchIndex(data, -1) {\n\t\t\t\tv := data[m[2]:m[3]]\n\t\t\t\tif placeholder(v) || entropy(v) < entropyThreshold {\n\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\temit(Finding{ru.id, line, base + m[0] + 1})\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\thit := false\n\t\tfor _, k := range ru.kw {\n\t\t\tif bytes.Contains(data, k) {\n\t\t\t\thit = true\n\t\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tif !hit {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, m := range ru.re.FindAllIndex(data, -1) {\n\t\t\temit(Finding{ru.id, line, base + m[0] + 1})\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\npackage redact\n\nimport (\n\t\"bufio\"\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"log/slog\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\nconst (\n\tDefaultMaxBytes  int64 = 1 << 20\n\tDefaultHeadBytes int64 = 32 << 10\n\tdefaultFindings        = 100\n)\n\n// ErrBadOptions is returned by New for inconsistent cap options.\nvar ErrBadOptions = errors.New(\"redact: bad options\")\n\n// Options tune a Redactor. Zero values select the defaults.\ntype Options struct {\n\tMaxBytes    int64   // default DefaultMaxBytes (1 MiB)\n\tHeadBytes   int64   // default DefaultHeadBytes (32 KiB)\n\tScanner     Scanner // default Builtin()\n\tMaxFindings int     // default 100; extra hits are counted, not stored\n}\n\n// Redactor is immutable after New and safe for concurrent use.\ntype Redactor struct {\n\tsecrets *Secrets\n\topts    Options\n\tchunk   int\n\topen    func(string) (io.ReadCloser, error) // test hook; default os.Open\n}\n\n// New returns a Redactor for the given known values.\nfunc New(s *Secrets, o Options) (*Redactor, error) {\n\tif s == nil {\n\t\ts = NewSecrets()\n\t}\n\tif o.MaxBytes == 0 {\n\t\to.MaxBytes = DefaultMaxBytes\n\t}\n\tif o.HeadBytes == 0 {\n\t\to.HeadBytes = DefaultHeadBytes\n\t}\n\tif o.Scanner == nil {\n\t\to.Scanner = Builtin()\n\t}\n\tif o.MaxFindings == 0 {\n\t\to.MaxFindings = defaultFindings\n\t}\n\tif o.MaxFindings < 0 {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"max findings %d: %w\", o.MaxFindings, ErrBadOptions)\n\t}\n\tif o.HeadBytes < 0 || o.MaxBytes < o.HeadBytes+markerReserve+1024 {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"max %d, head %d: %w\", o.MaxBytes, o.HeadBytes, ErrBadOptions)\n\t}\n\treturn &Redactor{secrets: s, opts: o, chunk: defaultChunk, open: func(p string) (io.ReadCloser, error) { return os.Open(p) }}, nil\n}\n\n// LogResult is the verdict for one log.\ntype LogResult struct {\n\tClean        []byte         // capped, redacted, scanned; nil unless the verdict is clean\n\tFlagged      bool           // a scan hit: commit nothing, add ard:needs-human\n\tFindings     []Finding      // from the full scan plus the re-scan, de-duplicated\n\tFindingCount int            // total, including findings beyond MaxFindings; saturates at the scanner cap (10000)\n\tReplaced     map[string]int // name -> count; for logging only\n\tTruncated    bool\n\tOrigBytes    int64  // raw size\n\tRedactedPath string // full redacted host-local log (dst)\n}\n\n// Log reads src (the raw combined log) and writes the full redacted log to dst\n// atomically (temp file in dst's directory, mode 0600, fsync, rename). On a\n// hit it returns Flagged with Clean == nil and a nil error. On any failure it\n// returns an error with a zero result; the caller treats that like a hit and\n// no dst or temp file is left behind.\nfunc (r *Redactor) Log(ctx context.Context, src, dst string) (LogResult, error) {\n\tstart := time.Now()\n\tin, err := r.open(src)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tdefer in.Close()\n\ttmp, err := os.CreateTemp(filepath.Dir(dst), \".redact-*.tmp\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: create temp: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\ttmpName := tmp.Name()\n\trenamed := false\n\tdefer func() {\n\t\tif !renamed {\n\t\t\ttmp.Close()\n\t\t\tos.Remove(tmpName)\n\t\t}\n\t}()\n\n\tbw := bufio.NewWriterSize(tmp, 64<<10)\n\tcounts, orig, err := r.secrets.stream(ctx, in, bw, r.chunk)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tif err := bw.Flush(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: flush: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tst, err := tmp.Stat()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: stat: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tif _, err := tmp.Seek(0, io.SeekStart); err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: seek: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tfound, err := r.scan(ctx, tmp)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: scan: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tif err := tmp.Sync(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: sync: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tif err := tmp.Close(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: close: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tif err := os.Rename(tmpName, dst); err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: rename: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\trenamed = true\n\tok := false\n\tdefer func() {\n\t\tif !ok {\n\t\t\tos.Remove(dst)\n\t\t}\n\t}()\n\n\tres := LogResult{Replaced: counts, OrigBytes: orig, RedactedPath: dst}\n\tif len(found) == 0 {\n\t\tf, err := os.Open(dst)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: open redacted: %w\", src, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tcapped, trunc, err := capReader(f, st.Size(), r.opts.MaxBytes, r.opts.HeadBytes)\n\t\tf.Close()\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: %w\", src, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tfound, err = r.scan(ctx, bytes.NewReader(capped))\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: rescan: %w\", src, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.Truncated = trunc\n\t\tif len(found) == 0 {\n\t\t\tres.Clean = capped\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tok = true\n\tres.Flagged = len(found) > 0\n\tres.FindingCount = len(found)\n\tres.Findings = limitFindings(found, r.opts.MaxFindings)\n\n\trules := make([]string, 0, len(res.Findings))\n\tfor _, f := range res.Findings {\n\t\trules = append(rules, f.Rule)\n\t}\n\tsort.Strings(rules)\n\tslog.Info(\"redact.log\", \"src\", src, \"orig_bytes\", res.OrigBytes, \"out_bytes\", len(res.Clean),\n\t\t\"replaced\", res.Replaced, \"findings\", res.FindingCount, \"rules\", rules,\n\t\t\"truncated\", res.Truncated, \"flagged\", res.Flagged, \"dur\", time.Since(start))\n\treturn res, nil\n}\n\n// TextResult is the verdict for a small text.\ntype TextResult struct {\n\tClean    []byte // nil when flagged\n\tFlagged  bool\n\tFindings []Finding\n}\n\n// Text redacts and scans b with no cap. Use it for role files, plan.md and\n// summary comments.\nfunc (r *Redactor) Text(ctx context.Context, b []byte) (TextResult, error) {\n\tout, _ := r.secrets.Bytes(b)\n\tfound, err := r.scan(ctx, bytes.NewReader(out))\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn TextResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact text: scan: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tif len(found) > 0 {\n\t\treturn TextResult{Flagged: true, Findings: limitFindings(found, r.opts.MaxFindings)}, nil\n\t}\n\treturn TextResult{Clean: out}, nil\n}\n\nfunc limitFindings(f []Finding, n int) []Finding {\n\tif len(f) > n {\n\t\treturn f[:n]\n\t}\n\treturn f\n}\n\n// scan runs the scanner so that a cancelled ctx fails closed even if the\n// scanner ignores it.\nfunc (r *Redactor) scan(ctx context.Context, rd io.Reader) ([]Finding, error) {\n\ttype out struct {\n\t\tf   []Finding\n\t\terr error\n\t}\n\tch := make(chan out, 1)\n\tgo func() {\n\t\tf, err := r.opts.Scanner.Scan(ctx, rd)\n\t\tch <- out{f, err}\n\t}()\n\tselect {\n\tcase o := <-ch:\n\t\treturn o.f, o.err\n\tcase <-ctx.Done():\n\t\treturn nil, ctx.Err()\n\t}\n}\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n7:ard (automated repository development) makes a GitHub repo maintain itself: scanners file deduplicated issues, triage agents turn each issue into a plan, a worker agent implements it on a branch, reviewer agents critique the PR, and a merger lands it once every review passes.\n13:- **Scanners** — every X (default 4) hours; find problems, file or update issues, close what is fixed\n26:    S[\"Scanners<br/>every X h; dedupe by fingerprint, group up to N\"] -->|ard:pending| T[\"Triage<br/>lead routes to PM, architect, designer, docs, eng\"]\n38:        D[\"State: controller db plus one SQLite db per scanner\"]\n72:| `ard:scanner:<name>` | Scanner | Which scanner owns the issue; used for dedupe and backpressure |\n73:| `ard:pending` | Scanner or human | Ready for triage; the lead's request label |\n85:Four more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on the issue and the PR, cleared by a green run), `ard:keep` (hold failed-job resources past 24 h) and `ard:pause` (on the pinned status issue, stops all new claims). `ard labels sync` creates and updates the full set from the resolved config, idempotently, and never deletes a label: stale `ard:` labels are only reported. In `label` mode a custom `intake.label` is part of the catalogue: sync creates it with a description saying it admits the issue to triage, and switching back to the default leaves the old custom label reported as stale, never deleted. `ard labels sync` and `ard config check` reject an intake label that is one of ard's own label names, naming `intake.label`; `ard serve` logs `config.intake_invalid` once per distinct value. `--repo owner/name` names the repository; when omitted, it is read from the `origin` remote. Role names are at most 31 characters and scanner names at most 38, because GitHub caps a label at 50 characters. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so branch names stay unambiguous.\n102:    issue.md             # scanner: the ask, findings, fingerprints, links\n127:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n154:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags.\n156:**One writer location per issue directory at a time.** Until the worker branch exists, `main` owns `.ard/<issue#>/`; scanners and triage write there. Once the branch is cut, the branch owns it; workers and reviewers commit to the PR, and a scanner with new findings comments on the issue instead. This keeps the PR merge free of conflicts in `.ard/`.\n164:## Scanners\n166:A scanner is a directory, `.ard/scanners/<name>/`, holding either an executable `scan` that prints findings as JSON lines or a `prompt.txt` for an LLM scanner. The controller does all dedupe, grouping, filing and closing, so deterministic tools and LLM scanners share one pipeline and no scanner holds a GitHub token.\n173:**The output schema is strict.** Each line is one JSON object with exactly these keys; blank lines are skipped. `fingerprint` (required, 1 to 512 bytes of valid UTF-8, no control characters), `title` (required, one line, at most 200 characters), `severity` (required: `critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low` or `info`), `group` (optional; empty means one issue per finding), `files` (optional repo-relative paths, clean, no `..`) and `body` (optional markdown, at most 64 KiB). An unknown key, a bad value, a duplicate fingerprint, a line over 1 MiB, a stream over 16 MiB or more than 10,000 findings is an invalid line, and **any invalid line fails the whole run**: nothing is filed, closed or changed, so a half-parsed run can never trigger autoclose. Titles, bodies and file names come from repository content, so the controller treats them as untrusted data: before they reach an issue body it escapes HTML comment delimiters, so scanner output cannot forge a marker.\n177:1. **Reconcile.** Fetch the scanner's open issues, plus a lookup of each issue that has a tracked finding and is no longer open, and update its state. Every marker line on an open issue becomes a row on that issue (a fingerprint on two open issues goes to the lowest number), so a crash between creating an issue and recording it heals here instead of filing a duplicate. Closed as completed: mark the fingerprints fixed, keeping a tombstone so a return can link the old issue. Closed as not planned or as a duplicate: suppress them for `stale.suppress_for` (default 90 days) from the close time. Deleted or transferred: drop. An issue that is reopened is tracked again and its suppression lifts. Reconcile also runs on a run that is then dropped, so state stays current.\n178:2. **Backpressure, in code, before the scanner starts.** The controller counts open issues with `ard:scanner:<name>`; at or above `max_open` it skips the run and logs it, autoclose scanners included: a scanner at its cap does not run with a zero filing budget, so it cannot autoclose its own fixed issues until a human or the stale closer frees a slot. A global cap across all scanners (`global_max_open`) stops a bad day from burying the queue. A skipped run is not queued: it counts as a run, and the next scheduled run checks again.\n179:3. **Scan.** Run the scanner on a read-only worktree of `origin/main`, with a timeout. It needs a free job slot, and scanners rank last for one. **A run that is denied a slot is not dropped: it is not recorded, stays due and is offered again on the next tick** (every five minutes), until it gets a slot or the controller stops. A failed scan (non-zero exit, timeout, invalid output) is recorded and changes nothing else.\n180:4. **Diff.** A new, unsuppressed fingerprint is queued for filing. A suppressed fingerprint stays quiet unless its severity rises above the suppressed one. A fingerprint that returns after being fixed files a new issue that links the old one as a regression. An open issue whose fingerprints are all gone gets \"No longer detected at `<sha>`\" and is closed, but only for scanners marked `autoclose` (deterministic ones); other scanners never edit or close an issue for absence. A returning finding unticks its line.\n181:5. **Group.** Findings that share a `group` key join one issue, up to `group_size` (N), as a checklist with one line per finding (ticked lines count towards N). An empty `group` means one issue per finding. New findings join an open group issue of the same group that has room and is not yet claimed, meaning it carries no `ard:` label besides `ard:scanner:<name>` and `ard:pending`; otherwise they start a new one. A regression never joins: it always opens a new issue, which may also hold new findings of the same group. The number of new issues per run is `min(max_open - open, global_max_open - global open)`; joins are free. Findings over that budget are filed most severe first, and the rest stay unrecorded so the next run offers them again.\n182:6. **File.** Edits and closes first, then joins, then new issues. Create the issue with `ard:scanner:<name>` and `ard:pending`, then queue `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` to the committer. The issue comes first because its number names the directory. A committer failure is logged and does not undo the issue: its body already carries the checklist. **An issue with `ard:hold` is never edited, joined, commented on or closed**, and a close waits while any `ard:<stage>:<role>:running` label is on the issue, so a running job is not orphaned. Edits never rewrite `issue.md`, because after a claim the branch may own `.ard/<issue#>/`.\n184:**A scanner loop runs at most one due scanner per tick.** The `scanners` loop polls every five minutes, picks the most overdue scanner (last run plus its `every`; a dropped run counts as a run) and runs it. Pausing stops it, because it files and closes issues. Its tick timeout is the longest scan timeout plus 15 minutes, not the poll interval.\n186:**Each scanner has its own state, and GitHub is the truth.** Every scanner keeps a separate SQLite file, `~/.ard/state/<owner>/<repo>/<scanner>.db`: fingerprint to issue, first and last seen, suppressions, last run. Scanners never share or lock each other's state, and one can be reset without touching the rest. The controller keeps its own jobs, attempts, events and a small key/value table (for example the status issue number) in `_controller.db` in the same directory, and dry-run uses `~/.ard/state-dryrun/<owner>/<repo>/`. Owner and repo are lowercased (GitHub names are case-insensitive) and nested, so distinct repos never share a directory (`a-b/c` and `a/b-c` would collide as `a-b-c`); `ard serve` and `ard rebuild-state` refuse to start while a pre-#82 flat `<owner>-<repo>` state directory exists under `state/` or `state-dryrun/`, and ard does not migrate it (decided, #82). Every issue body carries hidden markers, so `ard rebuild-state <scanner>` restores a database from GitHub after a lost disk or a host move. The body holds a header `<!-- ard:scanner=<name> group=<enc> -->` and a checklist between `<!-- ard:findings -->` and `<!-- /ard:findings -->`, one line per finding: `- [ ] <title> <!-- ard:fp=<enc> sev=<severity> [reg=<old issue>] -->`. `<enc>` percent-encodes every byte outside `[A-Za-z0-9._:/@+=,~-]`, so the proposal's example fingerprint stays readable and nothing can close the comment. A ticked box means the finding is gone. Per-finding details sit in a second span, `<!-- ard:details -->`, cut to 2,000 characters each and trimmed from the end to keep the body under 60,000; `issue.md` holds them untruncated. The controller reads and rewrites only the two spans, so text a human or triage adds elsewhere survives. A human who deletes a marker line makes that finding look new, which is accepted because GitHub is the truth.\n188:`ard rebuild-state <scanner>` takes the leader lock (it refuses to run while `ard serve` holds it), reads every issue with `ard:scanner:<name>`, open and closed, and replaces the scanner's findings and suppressions in one transaction, keeping the run history. For each fingerprint, an open issue wins (the lowest number), else the highest-numbered closed issue decides: completed leaves a `fixed` tombstone, not planned or duplicate leaves none. Independently, the highest-numbered not-planned or duplicate close gives a suppression until `closed_at + stale.suppress_for`, kept even when expired. It prints what it adds, removes and changes; with `--dry-run` it prints and writes nothing. The dry-run state database holds synthetic issues that are not on GitHub, so it is never rebuilt. Only scanners with a `scan` executable can be rebuilt. One known gap: reopening an old issue that shares a fingerprint with a newer one can leave the database different from a rebuild until the next rebuild-state.\n190:**Dry-run files nothing.** Under `--dry-run` a would-be issue gets a synthetic negative number, stored in the scanner's dry-run database like a real one, so repeated scans do not re-propose it and `max_open` is reached from synthetic issues. The committer is never called for a synthetic number. The global cap counts only the running scanner's synthetic issues, since scanners share no state.\n194:**LLM scanners need stable fingerprints.** Their prose changes run to run, so they fingerprint on rule, file and symbol, never on wording. Before filing, the controller also compares the title against the scanner's open issues and skips near-duplicates.\n196:**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a comment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any author other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppresses every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\n198:| Scanner | Engine | Every | Group size | Auto-close |\n200:| cve | osv-scanner and govulncheck (trivy and the Dependabot alerts API later) | 6 h | 1 | yes |\n209:**The cve scanner** (`scanners/cve`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, no build step) merges osv-scanner (all ecosystems) and govulncheck (Go reachability). Its fingerprint is `osv:<advisory>:<manifest>:<package>`, where the advisory is the smallest `GHSA-` id of the vulnerability, else the smallest `CVE-` id, else the smallest id, so one vulnerability reported under `GO-`, `GHSA-` and `CVE-` ids is one finding. Severity is `critical|high|medium|low`, from the CVSS score, else the database severity, else `medium`; a vulnerability govulncheck finds unreachable is capped at `medium`. Manifests under `testdata/`, `vendor/` and `node_modules/` are skipped. It fails closed: if a Go module cannot be analysed, the run fails and files nothing, unless a human adds a `no-govulncheck` file to the role directory, which skips govulncheck and reports reachability as unknown. Each Go module is analysed with the image's Go toolchain unless its `go` directive, or a `toolchain` line, needs a newer one. Then the scanner lets Go pick that toolchain (`GOTOOLCHAIN=auto` rules: the `toolchain` line if present, else the `go` directive's minimum version), downloads it from the Go module proxy (checksum-verified; scanner jobs have egress) into its temp dir, rebuilds the same govulncheck version with it, and scans with both. The image sets `TMPDIR=/var/tmp` because the executor's `/tmp` is noexec. If the temp dir is noexec, or the toolchain cannot be downloaded or built, the run fails closed and names the reason. Findings from such a module say which toolchain was scanned (`Go toolchain:` line), and standard-library findings are measured against it (for `go 1.27`, `go1.27.0`), not against the image's Go. The Dependabot alerts API is not wrapped, because a scanner holds no token.\n219:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the issue, its timeline and the labeler's permission, and it fails closed. Issues authored by the App (scanner-filed) always pass. In `all` mode only issues opened after `all` mode was first enabled enter without a label; older issues still need the label. In `label` mode only the most recent `labeled` event for the label counts: it must come from the App or from a collaborator with role triage, write, maintain or admin (matched case-insensitively), and an earlier authorised event does not rescue a later unauthorised one. When the label is present but not honoured, the controller adds `ard:needs-human` with a short comment asking a maintainer to re-apply the label. The loop records each admission in the state `admissions` table (`labeled_by`, `labeled_at`, `body_hash`); if the title or body is edited after admission, the issue is re-gated with `ard:needs-human` and a comment until a maintainer re-applies the intake label. App-authored issues are exempt from the hash check. The loop never waits for a job: each claimed job is handed to a watcher goroutine that closes out through the shared Finisher, and a restart leaves running jobs to pool adoption and the Reaper. The re-gate hash covers the title and the body without ard's own cost block (see Guardrails, \"Intake is configurable\"), so ard's table writes never re-gate an issue. The first time ard writes the block, the stored hash stays valid; a maintainer relabel that falls between a human edit and ard's next table write may need repeating, because an ard body write bumps the issue's `updated_at`, which fails safe.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92566d09-cc51-4307-8584-ef00e4a9d8c9","uuid":"f47a8fbc-63d9-48f5-b191-00867aeff196","timestamp":"2026-11-23T13:18:53.542Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"package redact\n\nimport (\n\t\"bufio\"\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n)\n\n// Finding is one scanner hit. It never carries the matched text.\ntype Finding struct {\n\tRule string // rule ID, e.g. \"github-token\"\n\tLine int    // 1-based line in the scanned stream\n\tCol  int    // 1-based byte column\n}\n\n// Scanner looks for secrets in a stream. Implementations must fail closed:\n// return an error rather than a partial verdict.\ntype Scanner interface {\n\tScan(ctx context.Context, r io.Reader) ([]Finding, error)\n}\n\nconst (\n\tscanWindow     = 1 << 20\n\tscanOverlap    = 4 << 10\n\tmaxScanReports = 10000\n)\n\ntype builtin struct{}\n\n// Builtin returns the in-repo pattern scanner. It stores at most 10000\n// findings per scan.\nfunc Builtin() Scanner { return builtin{} }\n\nfunc (builtin) Scan(ctx context.Context, r io.Reader) ([]Finding, error) {\n\tvar out []Finding\n\ttype key struct {\n\t\trule      string\n\t\tline, col int\n\t}\n\tseen := map[key]bool{}\n\temit := func(f Finding) {\n\t\tk := key{f.Rule, f.Line, f.Col}\n\t\tif !seen[k] && len(out) < maxScanReports {\n\t\t\tseen[k] = true\n\t\t\tout = append(out, f)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tbr := bufio.NewReaderSize(r, scanWindow)\n\tline, consumed, iter := 1, 0, 0\n\tvar carry []byte\n\tfor {\n\t\titer++\n\t\tif iter%1000 == 0 || carry != nil {\n\t\t\tif err := ctx.Err(); err != nil {\n\t\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"scan: %w\", err)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tfrag, err := br.ReadSlice('\\n')\n\t\tif err != nil && err != bufio.ErrBufferFull && err != io.EOF {\n\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"scan: read: %w\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tlong := err == bufio.ErrBufferFull\n\t\tdata, base := frag, consumed\n\t\tif carry != nil {\n\t\t\tdata = append(append([]byte(nil), carry...), frag...)\n\t\t\tbase = consumed - len(carry)\n\t\t}\n\t\tif len(data) > 0 {\n\t\t\tscanLine(bytes.TrimRight(data, \"\\r\\n\"), line, base, emit)\n\t\t}\n\t\tconsumed += len(frag)\n\t\tif long {\n\t\t\tfrom := len(data) - scanOverlap\n\t\t\tif from < 0 {\n\t\t\t\tfrom = 0\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tcarry = append([]byte(nil), data[from:]...)\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tcarry, consumed = nil, 0\n\t\tif err == io.EOF {\n\t\t\treturn out, nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tline++\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc scanLine(data []byte, line, base int, emit func(Finding)) {\n\tfor i := range rules {\n\t\tru := &rules[i]\n\t\tif ru.generic {\n\t\t\tif bytes.IndexByte(data, ':') < 0 && bytes.IndexByte(data, '=') < 0 {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tfor _, m := range ru.re.FindAllSubmatchIndex(data, -1) {\n\t\t\t\tv := data[m[2]:m[3]]\n\t\t\t\tif placeholder(v) || entropy(v) < entropyThreshold {\n\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\temit(Finding{ru.id, line, base + m[0] + 1})\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\thit := false\n\t\tfor _, k := range ru.kw {\n\t\t\tif bytes.Contains(data, k) {\n\t\t\t\thit = true\n\t\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tif !hit {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, m := range ru.re.FindAllIndex(data, -1) {\n\t\t\temit(Finding{ru.id, line, base + m[0] + 1})\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\npackage redact\n\nimport (\n\t\"bufio\"\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"log/slog\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\nconst (\n\tDefaultMaxBytes  int64 = 1 << 20\n\tDefaultHeadBytes int64 = 32 << 10\n\tdefaultFindings        = 100\n)\n\n// ErrBadOptions is returned by New for inconsistent cap options.\nvar ErrBadOptions = errors.New(\"redact: bad options\")\n\n// Options tune a Redactor. Zero values select the defaults.\ntype Options struct {\n\tMaxBytes    int64   // default DefaultMaxBytes (1 MiB)\n\tHeadBytes   int64   // default DefaultHeadBytes (32 KiB)\n\tScanner     Scanner // default Builtin()\n\tMaxFindings int     // default 100; extra hits are counted, not stored\n}\n\n// Redactor is immutable after New and safe for concurrent use.\ntype Redactor struct {\n\tsecrets *Secrets\n\topts    Options\n\tchunk   int\n\topen    func(string) (io.ReadCloser, error) // test hook; default os.Open\n}\n\n// New returns a Redactor for the given known values.\nfunc New(s *Secrets, o Options) (*Redactor, error) {\n\tif s == nil {\n\t\ts = NewSecrets()\n\t}\n\tif o.MaxBytes == 0 {\n\t\to.MaxBytes = DefaultMaxBytes\n\t}\n\tif o.HeadBytes == 0 {\n\t\to.HeadBytes = DefaultHeadBytes\n\t}\n\tif o.Scanner == nil {\n\t\to.Scanner = Builtin()\n\t}\n\tif o.MaxFindings == 0 {\n\t\to.MaxFindings = defaultFindings\n\t}\n\tif o.MaxFindings < 0 {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"max findings %d: %w\", o.MaxFindings, ErrBadOptions)\n\t}\n\tif o.HeadBytes < 0 || o.MaxBytes < o.HeadBytes+markerReserve+1024 {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"max %d, head %d: %w\", o.MaxBytes, o.HeadBytes, ErrBadOptions)\n\t}\n\treturn &Redactor{secrets: s, opts: o, chunk: defaultChunk, open: func(p string) (io.ReadCloser, error) { return os.Open(p) }}, nil\n}\n\n// LogResult is the verdict for one log.\ntype LogResult struct {\n\tClean        []byte         // capped, redacted, scanned; nil unless the verdict is clean\n\tFlagged      bool           // a scan hit: commit nothing, add ard:needs-human\n\tFindings     []Finding      // from the full scan plus the re-scan, de-duplicated\n\tFindingCount int            // total, including findings beyond MaxFindings; saturates at the scanner cap (10000)\n\tReplaced     map[string]int // name -> count; for logging only\n\tTruncated    bool\n\tOrigBytes    int64  // raw size\n\tRedactedPath string // full redacted host-local log (dst)\n}\n\n// Log reads src (the raw combined log) and writes the full redacted log to dst\n// atomically (temp file in dst's directory, mode 0600, fsync, rename). On a\n// hit it returns Flagged with Clean == nil and a nil error. On any failure it\n// returns an error with a zero result; the caller treats that like a hit and\n// no dst or temp file is left behind.\nfunc (r *Redactor) Log(ctx context.Context, src, dst string) (LogResult, error) {\n\tstart := time.Now()\n\tin, err := r.open(src)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tdefer in.Close()\n\ttmp, err := os.CreateTemp(filepath.Dir(dst), \".redact-*.tmp\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: create temp: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\ttmpName := tmp.Name()\n\trenamed := false\n\tdefer func() {\n\t\tif !renamed {\n\t\t\ttmp.Close()\n\t\t\tos.Remove(tmpName)\n\t\t}\n\t}()\n\n\tbw := bufio.NewWriterSize(tmp, 64<<10)\n\tcounts, orig, err := r.secrets.stream(ctx, in, bw, r.chunk)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tif err := bw.Flush(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: flush: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tst, err := tmp.Stat()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: stat: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tif _, err := tmp.Seek(0, io.SeekStart); err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: seek: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tfound, err := r.scan(ctx, tmp)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: scan: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tif err := tmp.Sync(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: sync: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tif err := tmp.Close(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: close: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\tif err := os.Rename(tmpName, dst); err != nil {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: rename: %w\", src, err)\n\t}\n\trenamed = true\n\tok := false\n\tdefer func() {\n\t\tif !ok {\n\t\t\tos.Remove(dst)\n\t\t}\n\t}()\n\n\tres := LogResult{Replaced: counts, OrigBytes: orig, RedactedPath: dst}\n\tif len(found) == 0 {\n\t\tf, err := os.Open(dst)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: open redacted: %w\", src, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tcapped, trunc, err := capReader(f, st.Size(), r.opts.MaxBytes, r.opts.HeadBytes)\n\t\tf.Close()\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: %w\", src, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tfound, err = r.scan(ctx, bytes.NewReader(capped))\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn LogResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact %s: rescan: %w\", src, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.Truncated = trunc\n\t\tif len(found) == 0 {\n\t\t\tres.Clean = capped\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tok = true\n\tres.Flagged = len(found) > 0\n\tres.FindingCount = len(found)\n\tres.Findings = limitFindings(found, r.opts.MaxFindings)\n\n\trules := make([]string, 0, len(res.Findings))\n\tfor _, f := range res.Findings {\n\t\trules = append(rules, f.Rule)\n\t}\n\tsort.Strings(rules)\n\tslog.Info(\"redact.log\", \"src\", src, \"orig_bytes\", res.OrigBytes, \"out_bytes\", len(res.Clean),\n\t\t\"replaced\", res.Replaced, \"findings\", res.FindingCount, \"rules\", rules,\n\t\t\"truncated\", res.Truncated, \"flagged\", res.Flagged, \"dur\", time.Since(start))\n\treturn res, nil\n}\n\n// TextResult is the verdict for a small text.\ntype TextResult struct {\n\tClean    []byte // nil when flagged\n\tFlagged  bool\n\tFindings []Finding\n}\n\n// Text redacts and scans b with no cap. Use it for role files, plan.md and\n// summary comments.\nfunc (r *Redactor) Text(ctx context.Context, b []byte) (TextResult, error) {\n\tout, _ := r.secrets.Bytes(b)\n\tfound, err := r.scan(ctx, bytes.NewReader(out))\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn TextResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"redact text: scan: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tif len(found) > 0 {\n\t\treturn TextResult{Flagged: true, Findings: limitFindings(found, r.opts.MaxFindings)}, nil\n\t}\n\treturn TextResult{Clean: out}, nil\n}\n\nfunc limitFindings(f []Finding, n int) []Finding {\n\tif len(f) > n {\n\t\treturn f[:n]\n\t}\n\treturn f\n}\n\n// scan runs the scanner so that a cancelled ctx fails closed even if the\n// scanner ignores it.\nfunc (r *Redactor) scan(ctx context.Context, rd io.Reader) ([]Finding, error) {\n\ttype out struct {\n\t\tf   []Finding\n\t\terr error\n\t}\n\tch := make(chan out, 1)\n\tgo func() {\n\t\tf, err := r.opts.Scanner.Scan(ctx, rd)\n\t\tch <- out{f, err}\n\t}()\n\tselect {\n\tcase o := <-ch:\n\t\treturn o.f, o.err\n\tcase <-ctx.Done():\n\t\treturn nil, ctx.Err()\n\t}\n}\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n7:ard (automated repository development) makes a GitHub repo maintain itself: scanners file deduplicated issues, triage agents turn each issue into a plan, a worker agent implements it on a branch, reviewer agents critique the PR, and a merger lands it once every review passes.\n13:- **Scanners** — every X (default 4) hours; find problems, file or update issues, close what is fixed\n26:    S[\"Scanners<br/>every X h; dedupe by fingerprint, group up to N\"] -->|ard:pending| T[\"Triage<br/>lead routes to PM, architect, designer, docs, eng\"]\n38:        D[\"State: controller db plus one SQLite db per scanner\"]\n72:| `ard:scanner:<name>` | Scanner | Which scanner owns the issue; used for dedupe and backpressure |\n73:| `ard:pending` | Scanner or human | Ready for triage; the lead's request label |\n85:Four more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on the issue and the PR, cleared by a green run), `ard:keep` (hold failed-job resources past 24 h) and `ard:pause` (on the pinned status issue, stops all new claims). `ard labels sync` creates and updates the full set from the resolved config, idempotently, and never deletes a label: stale `ard:` labels are only reported. In `label` mode a custom `intake.label` is part of the catalogue: sync creates it with a description saying it admits the issue to triage, and switching back to the default leaves the old custom label reported as stale, never deleted. `ard labels sync` and `ard config check` reject an intake label that is one of ard's own label names, naming `intake.label`; `ard serve` logs `config.intake_invalid` once per distinct value. `--repo owner/name` names the repository; when omitted, it is read from the `origin` remote. Role names are at most 31 characters and scanner names at most 38, because GitHub caps a label at 50 characters. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so branch names stay unambiguous.\n102:    issue.md             # scanner: the ask, findings, fingerprints, links\n127:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n154:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags.\n156:**One writer location per issue directory at a time.** Until the worker branch exists, `main` owns `.ard/<issue#>/`; scanners and triage write there. Once the branch is cut, the branch owns it; workers and reviewers commit to the PR, and a scanner with new findings comments on the issue instead. This keeps the PR merge free of conflicts in `.ard/`.\n164:## Scanners\n166:A scanner is a directory, `.ard/scanners/<name>/`, holding either an executable `scan` that prints findings as JSON lines or a `prompt.txt` for an LLM scanner. The controller does all dedupe, grouping, filing and closing, so deterministic tools and LLM scanners share one pipeline and no scanner holds a GitHub token.\n173:**The output schema is strict.** Each line is one JSON object with exactly these keys; blank lines are skipped. `fingerprint` (required, 1 to 512 bytes of valid UTF-8, no control characters), `title` (required, one line, at most 200 characters), `severity` (required: `critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low` or `info`), `group` (optional; empty means one issue per finding), `files` (optional repo-relative paths, clean, no `..`) and `body` (optional markdown, at most 64 KiB). An unknown key, a bad value, a duplicate fingerprint, a line over 1 MiB, a stream over 16 MiB or more than 10,000 findings is an invalid line, and **any invalid line fails the whole run**: nothing is filed, closed or changed, so a half-parsed run can never trigger autoclose. Titles, bodies and file names come from repository content, so the controller treats them as untrusted data: before they reach an issue body it escapes HTML comment delimiters, so scanner output cannot forge a marker.\n177:1. **Reconcile.** Fetch the scanner's open issues, plus a lookup of each issue that has a tracked finding and is no longer open, and update its state. Every marker line on an open issue becomes a row on that issue (a fingerprint on two open issues goes to the lowest number), so a crash between creating an issue and recording it heals here instead of filing a duplicate. Closed as completed: mark the fingerprints fixed, keeping a tombstone so a return can link the old issue. Closed as not planned or as a duplicate: suppress them for `stale.suppress_for` (default 90 days) from the close time. Deleted or transferred: drop. An issue that is reopened is tracked again and its suppression lifts. Reconcile also runs on a run that is then dropped, so state stays current.\n178:2. **Backpressure, in code, before the scanner starts.** The controller counts open issues with `ard:scanner:<name>`; at or above `max_open` it skips the run and logs it, autoclose scanners included: a scanner at its cap does not run with a zero filing budget, so it cannot autoclose its own fixed issues until a human or the stale closer frees a slot. A global cap across all scanners (`global_max_open`) stops a bad day from burying the queue. A skipped run is not queued: it counts as a run, and the next scheduled run checks again.\n179:3. **Scan.** Run the scanner on a read-only worktree of `origin/main`, with a timeout. It needs a free job slot, and scanners rank last for one. **A run that is denied a slot is not dropped: it is not recorded, stays due and is offered again on the next tick** (every five minutes), until it gets a slot or the controller stops. A failed scan (non-zero exit, timeout, invalid output) is recorded and changes nothing else.\n180:4. **Diff.** A new, unsuppressed fingerprint is queued for filing. A suppressed fingerprint stays quiet unless its severity rises above the suppressed one. A fingerprint that returns after being fixed files a new issue that links the old one as a regression. An open issue whose fingerprints are all gone gets \"No longer detected at `<sha>`\" and is closed, but only for scanners marked `autoclose` (deterministic ones); other scanners never edit or close an issue for absence. A returning finding unticks its line.\n181:5. **Group.** Findings that share a `group` key join one issue, up to `group_size` (N), as a checklist with one line per finding (ticked lines count towards N). An empty `group` means one issue per finding. New findings join an open group issue of the same group that has room and is not yet claimed, meaning it carries no `ard:` label besides `ard:scanner:<name>` and `ard:pending`; otherwise they start a new one. A regression never joins: it always opens a new issue, which may also hold new findings of the same group. The number of new issues per run is `min(max_open - open, global_max_open - global open)`; joins are free. Findings over that budget are filed most severe first, and the rest stay unrecorded so the next run offers them again.\n182:6. **File.** Edits and closes first, then joins, then new issues. Create the issue with `ard:scanner:<name>` and `ard:pending`, then queue `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` to the committer. The issue comes first because its number names the directory. A committer failure is logged and does not undo the issue: its body already carries the checklist. **An issue with `ard:hold` is never edited, joined, commented on or closed**, and a close waits while any `ard:<stage>:<role>:running` label is on the issue, so a running job is not orphaned. Edits never rewrite `issue.md`, because after a claim the branch may own `.ard/<issue#>/`.\n184:**A scanner loop runs at most one due scanner per tick.** The `scanners` loop polls every five minutes, picks the most overdue scanner (last run plus its `every`; a dropped run counts as a run) and runs it. Pausing stops it, because it files and closes issues. Its tick timeout is the longest scan timeout plus 15 minutes, not the poll interval.\n186:**Each scanner has its own state, and GitHub is the truth.** Every scanner keeps a separate SQLite file, `~/.ard/state/<owner>/<repo>/<scanner>.db`: fingerprint to issue, first and last seen, suppressions, last run. Scanners never share or lock each other's state, and one can be reset without touching the rest. The controller keeps its own jobs, attempts, events and a small key/value table (for example the status issue number) in `_controller.db` in the same directory, and dry-run uses `~/.ard/state-dryrun/<owner>/<repo>/`. Owner and repo are lowercased (GitHub names are case-insensitive) and nested, so distinct repos never share a directory (`a-b/c` and `a/b-c` would collide as `a-b-c`); `ard serve` and `ard rebuild-state` refuse to start while a pre-#82 flat `<owner>-<repo>` state directory exists under `state/` or `state-dryrun/`, and ard does not migrate it (decided, #82). Every issue body carries hidden markers, so `ard rebuild-state <scanner>` restores a database from GitHub after a lost disk or a host move. The body holds a header `<!-- ard:scanner=<name> group=<enc> -->` and a checklist between `<!-- ard:findings -->` and `<!-- /ard:findings -->`, one line per finding: `- [ ] <title> <!-- ard:fp=<enc> sev=<severity> [reg=<old issue>] -->`. `<enc>` percent-encodes every byte outside `[A-Za-z0-9._:/@+=,~-]`, so the proposal's example fingerprint stays readable and nothing can close the comment. A ticked box means the finding is gone. Per-finding details sit in a second span, `<!-- ard:details -->`, cut to 2,000 characters each and trimmed from the end to keep the body under 60,000; `issue.md` holds them untruncated. The controller reads and rewrites only the two spans, so text a human or triage adds elsewhere survives. A human who deletes a marker line makes that finding look new, which is accepted because GitHub is the truth.\n188:`ard rebuild-state <scanner>` takes the leader lock (it refuses to run while `ard serve` holds it), reads every issue with `ard:scanner:<name>`, open and closed, and replaces the scanner's findings and suppressions in one transaction, keeping the run history. For each fingerprint, an open issue wins (the lowest number), else the highest-numbered closed issue decides: completed leaves a `fixed` tombstone, not planned or duplicate leaves none. Independently, the highest-numbered not-planned or duplicate close gives a suppression until `closed_at + stale.suppress_for`, kept even when expired. It prints what it adds, removes and changes; with `--dry-run` it prints and writes nothing. The dry-run state database holds synthetic issues that are not on GitHub, so it is never rebuilt. Only scanners with a `scan` executable can be rebuilt. One known gap: reopening an old issue that shares a fingerprint with a newer one can leave the database different from a rebuild until the next rebuild-state.\n190:**Dry-run files nothing.** Under `--dry-run` a would-be issue gets a synthetic negative number, stored in the scanner's dry-run database like a real one, so repeated scans do not re-propose it and `max_open` is reached from synthetic issues. The committer is never called for a synthetic number. The global cap counts only the running scanner's synthetic issues, since scanners share no state.\n194:**LLM scanners need stable fingerprints.** Their prose changes run to run, so they fingerprint on rule, file and symbol, never on wording. Before filing, the controller also compares the title against the scanner's open issues and skips near-duplicates.\n196:**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a comment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any author other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppresses every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\n198:| Scanner | Engine | Every | Group size | Auto-close |\n200:| cve | osv-scanner and govulncheck (trivy and the Dependabot alerts API later) | 6 h | 1 | yes |\n209:**The cve scanner** (`scanners/cve`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, no build step) merges osv-scanner (all ecosystems) and govulncheck (Go reachability). Its fingerprint is `osv:<advisory>:<manifest>:<package>`, where the advisory is the smallest `GHSA-` id of the vulnerability, else the smallest `CVE-` id, else the smallest id, so one vulnerability reported under `GO-`, `GHSA-` and `CVE-` ids is one finding. Severity is `critical|high|medium|low`, from the CVSS score, else the database severity, else `medium`; a vulnerability govulncheck finds unreachable is capped at `medium`. Manifests under `testdata/`, `vendor/` and `node_modules/` are skipped. It fails closed: if a Go module cannot be analysed, the run fails and files nothing, unless a human adds a `no-govulncheck` file to the role directory, which skips govulncheck and reports reachability as unknown. Each Go module is analysed with the image's Go toolchain unless its `go` directive, or a `toolchain` line, needs a newer one. Then the scanner lets Go pick that toolchain (`GOTOOLCHAIN=auto` rules: the `toolchain` line if present, else the `go` directive's minimum version), downloads it from the Go module proxy (checksum-verified; scanner jobs have egress) into its temp dir, rebuilds the same govulncheck version with it, and scans with both. The image sets `TMPDIR=/var/tmp` because the executor's `/tmp` is noexec. If the temp dir is noexec, or the toolchain cannot be downloaded or built, the run fails closed and names the reason. Findings from such a module say which toolchain was scanned (`Go toolchain:` line), and standard-library findings are measured against it (for `go 1.27`, `go1.27.0`), not against the image's Go. The Dependabot alerts API is not wrapped, because a scanner holds no token.\n219:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the issue, its timeline and the labeler's permission, and it fails closed. Issues authored by the App (scanner-filed) always pass. In `all` mode only issues opened after `all` mode was first enabled enter without a label; older issues still need the label. In `label` mode only the most recent `labeled` event for the label counts: it must come from the App or from a collaborator with role triage, write, maintain or admin (matched case-insensitively), and an earlier authorised event does not rescue a later unauthorised one. When the label is present but not honoured, the controller adds `ard:needs-human` with a short comment asking a maintainer to re-apply the label. The loop records each admission in the state `admissions` table (`labeled_by`, `labeled_at`, `body_hash`); if the title or body is edited after admission, the issue is re-gated with `ard:needs-human` and a comment until a maintainer re-applies the intake label. App-authored issues are exempt from the hash check. The loop never waits for a job: each claimed job is handed to a watcher goroutine that closes out through the shared Finisher, and a restart leaves running jobs to pool adoption and the Reaper. The re-gate hash covers the title and the body without ard's own cost block (see Guardrails, \"Intake is configurable\"), so ard's table writes never re-gate an issue. The first time ard writes the block, the stored hash stays valid; a maintainer relabel that falls between a human edit and ard's next table write may need repeating, because an ard body write bumps the issue's `updated_at`, which fails safe.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HhfewuBws3QkfY